A||||3||the need for speed||front spring how do i remove it on an 87||||1||
Z||000000||the need for speed||10-25-2000||03:11 PM||||front spring how do i remove it? i tryed to look it up in the hayns book but didnt see anything want to put new eibach up there any info       thanks for the help||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||10-25-2000||03:41 PM||||You'll need to get a ball joint "Pickle fork" tool or a ball joint seperator.  You'll need to support the car on jack stands and support the lower control arm with a jack and to be safe use a coil compressor to hold and compress and relieve the spring.<P>Loosen the lower control arm ball joint nut, but do not remove it so that it's still on the threads.  Split the ball joint.  Remove the bolt and release the jack slowly.<P>Compress the new spring with coil compressors, put the coil in, jack up the lower arm, reconnect the lower ball joint, torque accordingly to specs.  Remove the compressors, and lower the jack.<P>This can be a dangerous job if it's not done correctly, you might want to have a friend or a mechanic help you.  I haven't removed coils on a Fiero yet, this is sort of a general guide.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||10-25-2000||10:00 PM||||Raise car 16" high and place on jackstands.<BR>place hydralic jack under control arm.<BR>wrap a saftey chain around spring and frame.<BR>seperate lower balljoint with pickle fork.<BR>SLOWLY lower hydralic jack until control arm is all the way down. <BR>Use a pry bar or long screwdriver to push spring off of control arm. (there is very little tension on the spring) <BR>Place Eibach spring into area . (Spring is shorter so it's real easy to put on.)<BR>jack up control arm (don't forget saftey chain!) <BR>reattach ball joint. (If you damaged boot when taking it apart replace boot before re- assembly)<BR>This is how I did mine. With the 84-87 you do not need a spring compressor. The saftey chain is just in case you lower jack too fast.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||10-26-2000||10:42 PM||||Orief is right, you don't need a spring compressor. If you're careful and have done this a time or two, you don't need the safety chain either. Support the lower control arm solidly with a jack and like he said, lower slowly after the ball joint is separated.||||reg||1
